 GE theory from the \"Anything goes\" theorem may fail. Now, the question the researcher wants to ask is:"]}, "id": "BB_2", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 18.5, "y": 44.4, "width": 62.4, "height": 22.5, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 18.5, "y": 44.4, "width": 62.4, "height": 22.5, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 18.5, "y": 44.4, "width": 62.4, "height": 22.5, "rotation": 0, "text": ["\"Given a set of observations on prices and individual endowments {p^t, {\u03c9_i^t}i=1}_t=1^T, does there exist a sequence of pure trade economies Et = {(R_+^L,  \u227f_i, \u03c9_i^t)}_i=1^I with aggregate excess demand function z_E_t (p^t) such that z_E_t (p^t) = 0 for all t = 1, 2, ..., T?"]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 14.2, "y": 69.4, "width": 71.1, "height": 21.9, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 14.2, "y": 69.4, "width": 71.1, "height": 21.9, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 14.2, "y": 69.4, "width": 71.1, "height": 21.9, "rotation": 0, "text": ["We now allow the data to come from different economies (they are indexed by t). The variability in the endowment vector data mechanically changes the economy. However, preferences are the same for all economies Et, and the source of variability is the different endowment profiles we might see in the data."]}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 31.640625, "y": 0.8223684210526315, "width": 17.393092105263158, "height": 2.796052631578948, "rotation": 0}, "id": "OyPC_dm3fn", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "manual"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 31.640625, "y": 0.8223684210526315, "width": 17.393092105263158, "height": 2.796052631578948, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "OyPC_dm3fn", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "manual"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 31.640625, "y": 0.8223684210526315, "width": 17.393092105263158, "height": 2.796052631578948, "rotation": 0, "text": ["Falsifiability of General Models"]}, "id": "OyPC_dm3fn", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "manual"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 17.300575657894736, "y": 96.71052631578947, "width": 15.265213815789476, "height": 2.302631578947384, "rotation": 0}, "id": "QJNPPrH0Kf", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "manual"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 17.300575657894736, "y": 96.71052631578947, "width": 15.265213815789476, "height": 2.302631578947384, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "QJNPPrH0Kf", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "manua